-- MRCP Part 2: Psychiatry (batch 1)
-- 50 questions covering anxiety & somatic disorders, capacity & the Mental Health Act,
-- mood disorders, psychosis, substance misuse.

  'draft', 'core',
  'A 29-year-old woman with a two-year history of panic disorder with agoraphobia has completed a 12-week course of sertraline titrated to 150 mg daily, combined with weekly CBT, with only partial improvement in panic frequency. She has no cardiac or hepatic disease and takes no other regular medication. She asks about further options as she remains unable to travel on public transport.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['panic disorder','SSRI non-response','venlafaxine']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 'mrcp2', '7bb7f76b-1256-46d8-9a19-786810963cbf',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 44-year-old woman with generalised anxiety disorder has been taking sertraline 100 mg daily for six weeks. She presents with headache, nausea, lethargy and confusion. Blood tests show sodium 118 mmol/L (previously 138 mmol/L one month ago), with normal renal function, normal cortisol, and urine osmolality inappropriately high relative to a low plasma osmolality. She takes no diuretics.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['SSRI-induced hyponatraemia','SIADH','mirtazapine']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003', 'mrcp2', '7bb7f76b-1256-46d8-9a19-786810963cbf',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 38-year-old man has attended the emergency department fourteen times in the past year with chest and abdominal symptoms, each time undergoing extensive investigation that has been unremarkable, including two prior normal CT scans, endoscopy and cardiac stress testing. He describes persistent, disproportionate worry that he has an undiagnosed cancer despite repeated reassurance, spends several hours daily checking his body for lumps, and has stopped working. He has no depressive or psychotic symptoms.',
  'What is the most appropriate management approach?',
  array['illness anxiety disorder','health anxiety','CBT']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000004', 'mrcp2', '7bb7f76b-1256-46d8-9a19-786810963cbf',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 26-year-old woman is admitted under neurology with recurrent episodes of asymmetric limb jerking and unresponsiveness lasting several minutes, with eyes forcefully closed during episodes and preserved awareness reported afterwards on some occasions. Video-EEG telemetry during three witnessed events shows no epileptiform activity. She has a history of childhood emotional trauma and was recently started on levetiracetam by a previous team, which has not reduced episode frequency.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['functional neurological disorder','dissociative seizures','psychoeducation']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000005', 'mrcp2', '7bb7f76b-1256-46d8-9a19-786810963cbf',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 51-year-old woman has a five-year history of widespread pain, fatigue and cognitive fog, extensively investigated by rheumatology and neurology with normal inflammatory markers, autoimmune screen, and MRI. She meets criteria for somatic symptom disorder with predominant pain. She is due to be seen in a general medical clinic for ongoing symptom management after her third negative work-up this year, and has previously found that clinicians ordering new tests briefly reduces her anxiety but she returns within weeks requesting further investigations.',
  'What is the most appropriate long-term management strategy?',
  array['somatic symptom disorder','chronic pain','collaborative care']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000006', 'mrcp2', '7bb7f76b-1256-46d8-9a19-786810963cbf',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 35-year-old paramedic develops recurrent intrusive memories, nightmares, hypervigilance and avoidance of ambulances three months after attending a mass casualty road traffic incident. Symptoms are causing significant occupational impairment. He has no past psychiatric history and does not drink alcohol excessively.',
  'What is the most appropriate first-line treatment?',
  array['PTSD','trauma-focused CBT','EMDR']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000007', 'mrcp2', '7bb7f76b-1256-46d8-9a19-786810963cbf',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 24-year-old man with obsessive-compulsive disorder involving contamination fears and hand-washing rituals lasting several hours daily has shown minimal improvement after 12 weeks of sertraline at the maximum tolerated licensed dose alongside exposure and response prevention therapy. He has no cardiac history and a baseline ECG is normal.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['treatment-resistant OCD','clomipramine','SSRI augmentation']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008', 'mrcp2', '7bb7f76b-1256-46d8-9a19-786810963cbf',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 19-year-old woman with body dysmorphic disorder is preoccupied with perceived asymmetry of her nose, checking mirrors for over three hours daily and avoiding university lectures. She has researched private rhinoplasty and asks her GP for an urgent surgical referral, stating this is the only thing that will help.',
  'What is the most appropriate initial management?',
  array['body dysmorphic disorder','SSRI','cosmetic surgery avoidance']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000009', 'mrcp2', '7bb7f76b-1256-46d8-9a19-786810963cbf',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 58-year-old woman on long-term sertraline for anxiety is prescribed tramadol for acute back pain by an out-of-hours service. Twelve hours later she develops agitation, diaphoresis, tremor, hyperreflexia and clonus in both lower limbs, with a temperature of 38.6°C and heart rate 128/min. She is alert but anxious.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['serotonin syndrome','SSRI-tramadol interaction','cyproheptadine']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000010', 'mrcp2', '7bb7f76b-1256-46d8-9a19-786810963cbf',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 62-year-old woman has taken diazepam 10 mg three times daily for generalised anxiety disorder for the past eleven years, originally prescribed after a bereavement. She now wishes to stop, reporting that it no longer controls her anxiety effectively and she is concerned about long-term cognitive effects. She has no history of seizures.',
  'What is the most appropriate approach to discontinuation?',
  array['benzodiazepine dependence','gradual tapering','withdrawal seizure risk']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011', 'mrcp2', '18154b90-88c8-40fc-95fe-72730c531ef5',
  'draft', 'core',
  $x$An 82-year-old woman with known moderate Alzheimer's dementia is admitted with a necrotic diabetic foot ulcer and systemic sepsis. The surgical team recommend below-knee amputation as life-saving. When asked, she says she understands she is unwell but repeatedly states 'I just want to go home, leave my leg alone' and cannot explain what would happen if the infection is not treated, nor recall this conversation minutes later.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate next step in assessing her capacity to make this decision?',
  array['Mental Capacity Act','two-stage test','decision-specific capacity']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012', 'mrcp2', '18154b90-88c8-40fc-95fe-72730c531ef5',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 76-year-old man with vascular dementia is confirmed, after formal assessment, to lack capacity to decide about starting antibiotics and possible surgical debridement for a spreading cellulitis. He has two children who disagree with each other about what he would have wanted, and no advance decision or lasting power of attorney is in place.',
  'What is the most appropriate approach to making this treatment decision?',
  array['best interests','Mental Capacity Act','family involvement']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013', 'mrcp2', '18154b90-88c8-40fc-95fe-72730c531ef5',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 70-year-old man on an acute medical ward has an acute confusional state secondary to a urinary tract infection. He is agitated, trying to leave the ward pulling out his cannula, and states he wants to go home despite being intermittently drowsy, disoriented in time and place, and unable to retain information given to him minutes earlier. It is out of hours and no psychiatrist or Mental Health Act assessors are immediately available, but he requires urgent IV antibiotics and monitoring.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate legal framework to prevent him leaving and enable urgent treatment?',
  array['Section 5(2)','delirium','Mental Capacity Act emergency treatment']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014', 'mrcp2', '18154b90-88c8-40fc-95fe-72730c531ef5',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 45-year-old woman with severe depression and psychotic symptoms (nihilistic delusions that her internal organs have stopped functioning) has refused all oral intake for eight days and is now dehydrated with an acute kidney injury. She lacks capacity to make decisions about treatment due to her mental disorder and her risk to her own health is assessed as high. Her family wants her treated urgently, including consideration of ECT, but she is refusing admission.',
  'What is the most appropriate legal route to admit and treat her?',
  array['Section 3 Mental Health Act','detention for treatment','severe depression']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015', 'mrcp2', '18154b90-88c8-40fc-95fe-72730c531ef5',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'An 84-year-old woman with advanced dementia is admitted to a medical ward for treatment of pneumonia. She is confused, attempts to leave repeatedly, and the ward has locked doors with staff redirecting her whenever she approaches the exit; she is also assessed as lacking capacity to consent to remaining in hospital for treatment. The team judge these restrictions are necessary and proportionate for her safety and are likely to continue for the full duration of her admission.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step regarding the legal framework for her ongoing restriction?',
  array['deprivation of liberty safeguards','Liberty Protection Safeguards','continuous supervision and control']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016', 'mrcp2', '18154b90-88c8-40fc-95fe-72730c531ef5',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  $x$A 47-year-old Jehovah's Witness man is brought in unconscious after a massive upper gastrointestinal haemorrhage with a haemoglobin of 42 g/L and haemodynamic instability. His wife produces a signed, witnessed advance decision document, dated eighteen months ago while he had capacity, explicitly refusing blood and blood products under any circumstances, including life-threatening haemorrhage, and specifying his diagnosis of peptic ulcer disease as a relevant example.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate approach to transfusion in this situation?',
  array['advance decision to refuse treatment','validity and applicability','blood transfusion']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000017', 'mrcp2', '18154b90-88c8-40fc-95fe-72730c531ef5',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 33-year-old woman detained under Section 3 of the Mental Health Act for treatment of severe psychotic depression develops acute appendicitis requiring emergency appendicectomy. She lacks capacity to consent to the surgery due to her acute mental state, and there is no time to await a second opinion appointed doctor (SOAD) certificate that would ordinarily be required for treatment beyond the initial three-month period under Part 4 of the Act.',
  'What is the most appropriate legal basis for proceeding with the emergency appendicectomy?',
  array['Section 62 Mental Health Act','urgent treatment','Part 4 consent to treatment']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000018', 'mrcp2', '18154b90-88c8-40fc-95fe-72730c531ef5',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 79-year-old man with fluctuating delirium secondary to pneumonia is due to undergo an elective decision about long-term anticoagulation for newly diagnosed atrial fibrillation. On the morning ward round he appears lucid, understands and repeats back the risks and benefits clearly, and expresses a clear, consistent preference. By the afternoon, during a period of worsening confusion, he cannot recall the conversation.',
  'What is the most appropriate approach to this capacity assessment?',
  array['fluctuating capacity','delirium','timing of assessment']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000019', 'mrcp2', '18154b90-88c8-40fc-95fe-72730c531ef5',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 17-year-old girl with severe anorexia nervosa (BMI 12.8 kg/m2, bradycardia 38/min, potassium 2.9 mmol/L) is refusing nasogastric feeding and any further treatment, stating she does not believe she is unwell despite objective evidence of life-threatening malnutrition. Psychiatric assessment concludes she lacks capacity to weigh this decision due to her eating disorder distorting her perception of risk.',
  'What is the most appropriate legal route to provide life-saving nasogastric feeding against her wishes?',
  array['anorexia nervosa','Mental Health Act treatment for physical consequences','detention']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000020', 'mrcp2', '18154b90-88c8-40fc-95fe-72730c531ef5',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 39-year-old man detained under Section 3 of the Mental Health Act for treatment of schizophrenia has been granted Section 17 leave to attend a medical outpatient appointment for management of clozapine-associated constipation, accompanied by a nurse escort. He absconds from the escort in the hospital car park and does not return. Twenty-four hours later he has not been located.',
  'What is the most appropriate action regarding his legal status?',
  array['Section 17 leave','absent without leave','return to hospital']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000021', 'mrcp2', 'f01d90fc-3d21-47da-a251-be3027d28eda',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 68-year-old woman with bipolar affective disorder maintained on lithium 800 mg at night (stable trough level 0.7 mmol/L for years) is admitted with a two-week history of diarrhoea and vomiting from gastroenteritis, treated by her GP with regular ibuprofen for associated abdominal cramps and started on bendroflumethiazide for newly noted hypertension four days ago. She now has coarse tremor, ataxia, confusion and vomiting. Lithium level is 2.8 mmol/L, creatinine is markedly elevated from baseline.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['lithium toxicity','precipitating drug interactions','haemodialysis']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000022', 'mrcp2', 'f01d90fc-3d21-47da-a251-be3027d28eda',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 46-year-old man with major depressive disorder has failed to respond adequately to two sequential adequate trials of different SSRIs (sertraline then escitalopram), each at maximum tolerated dose for at least 8 weeks, alongside CBT. He remains significantly depressed with poor sleep, appetite and concentration, though not actively suicidal.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in pharmacological management?',
  array['treatment-resistant depression','augmentation strategy','lithium augmentation']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000023', 'mrcp2', 'f01d90fc-3d21-47da-a251-be3027d28eda',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 54-year-old woman with severe depression has profound psychomotor retardation, is mute, holds fixed odd postures for prolonged periods, exhibits waxy flexibility on examination, and has refused oral intake for five days, now with significant dehydration. A lorazepam 2 mg IV challenge produces marked, though temporary, improvement in her responsiveness and mobility.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['catatonia','lorazepam challenge','ECT for catatonia']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000024', 'mrcp2', 'f01d90fc-3d21-47da-a251-be3027d28eda',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 31-year-old man with known bipolar I disorder, previously well-controlled on lithium, presents with three days of markedly elevated mood, rapid pressured speech, grandiose plans to invest his savings, and having slept only two hours per night without fatigue. He recently stopped his lithium two weeks ago against medical advice. His current medications otherwise include none, and physical examination and basic bloods (including thyroid function) are unremarkable.',
  'What is the most appropriate acute management?',
  array['acute mania','antipsychotic treatment','lithium restart']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000025', 'mrcp2', 'f01d90fc-3d21-47da-a251-be3027d28eda',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 27-year-old woman is admitted to a mother and baby psychiatric unit six days postpartum with abrupt-onset agitation, insomnia, disorganised behaviour, and fluctuating grandiose and paranoid ideas, rapidly escalating over 48 hours. She has no past psychiatric history. Her partner reports she has been expressing fears that hospital staff intend to harm the baby.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management approach?',
  array['postpartum psychosis','psychiatric emergency','mother and baby unit']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000026', 'mrcp2', 'f01d90fc-3d21-47da-a251-be3027d28eda',
  'draft', 'core',
  $x$A 40-year-old man abruptly stops paroxetine, which he had been taking for major depressive disorder for the past year, after running out of his prescription while on holiday. Three days later he develops dizziness, 'electric shock' sensations in his head, nausea, irritability and vivid dreams. He is otherwise medically well.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate management?',
  array['antidepressant discontinuation syndrome','paroxetine','gradual tapering']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000027', 'mrcp2', 'f01d90fc-3d21-47da-a251-be3027d28eda',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 29-year-old woman with bipolar I disorder, currently well-controlled on sodium valproate, discovers she is six weeks pregnant. She is euthymic and has had no manic or depressive episodes for eighteen months. She asks about the safety of continuing her current treatment.',
  'What is the most appropriate management of her mood stabiliser during pregnancy?',
  array['valproate teratogenicity','bipolar disorder in pregnancy','medication switch']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000028', 'mrcp2', 'f01d90fc-3d21-47da-a251-be3027d28eda',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 35-year-old woman with bipolar disorder has had four distinct mood episodes (two depressive, two hypomanic) within the past twelve months despite adherence to sertraline monotherapy, which was originally started for a depressive episode and continued by her GP. She is currently mildly depressed with no current risk features.',
  'What is the most appropriate change in management?',
  array['rapid cycling bipolar disorder','antidepressant monotherapy avoidance','mood stabiliser']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000029', 'mrcp2', 'f01d90fc-3d21-47da-a251-be3027d28eda',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  $x$A 52-year-old man with bipolar disorder presents with a mixed affective episode: he describes profound low mood, hopelessness and suicidal ideation, but on examination has pressured speech, psychomotor agitation, racing thoughts and has not slept in three days. He is currently prescribed only sertraline, started by a locum GP two weeks ago for 'depression'.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate immediate change in management?',
  array['mixed affective episode','antidepressant avoidance','suicide risk in mixed states']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000030', 'mrcp2', 'f01d90fc-3d21-47da-a251-be3027d28eda',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 60-year-old man with a long-standing history of recurrent severe depressive episodes and two previous serious suicide attempts is reviewed in clinic. He has had five depressive relapses over the past ten years despite trials of two SSRIs and venlafaxine, each discontinued after 6-12 months of stability, followed by relapse each time medication was withdrawn. He is currently euthymic on venlafaxine and asks about long-term relapse prevention given his suicide history.',
  'Which additional long-term treatment has the strongest evidence for reducing suicide risk in this context?',
  array['suicide risk reduction','lithium augmentation','long-term relapse prevention']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000031', 'mrcp2', '18394338-9140-4950-8963-d1f4e543e1f8',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 34-year-old man with treatment-resistant schizophrenia started clozapine six weeks ago. Routine full blood count shows a neutrophil count of 1.3 x10^9/L (previously 3.8 x10^9/L two weeks ago), with no fever, sore throat or other signs of infection. He feels well.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['clozapine-induced neutropenia','agranulocytosis monitoring','stopping clozapine']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000032', 'mrcp2', '18394338-9140-4950-8963-d1f4e543e1f8',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 41-year-old man with schizophrenia on long-term haloperidol depot presents with fever (39.4°C), profound generalised muscle rigidity, confusion, and autonomic instability with labile blood pressure and tachycardia. Creatine kinase is markedly elevated at 12,000 U/L. His last depot injection was four days ago; no dose change or new medication has occurred.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['neuroleptic malignant syndrome','dantrolene','stopping antipsychotic']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000033', 'mrcp2', '18394338-9140-4950-8963-d1f4e543e1f8',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 29-year-old woman started clozapine three weeks ago for treatment-resistant schizophrenia, with the dose titrated according to protocol. She now reports chest pain, palpitations and mild breathlessness, with a resting tachycardia of 118/min, troponin elevated at 340 ng/L (upper limit of normal 14 ng/L), and CRP markedly raised. ECG shows sinus tachycardia with non-specific ST changes; there is no history of cocaine use or recent infective symptoms.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate action?',
  array['clozapine-induced myocarditis','troponin monitoring','stopping clozapine']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000034', 'mrcp2', '18394338-9140-4950-8963-d1f4e543e1f8',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 55-year-old man is acutely agitated and psychotic on the medical ward following alcohol withdrawal complications, requiring IV haloperidol for rapid tranquillisation. Prior to administration, a baseline ECG shows a QTc of 470 ms. He has no other cardiac history.',
  'What is the most appropriate approach to using antipsychotic medication in this situation?',
  array['QTc prolongation','IV haloperidol risk','antipsychotic choice in cardiac risk']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000035', 'mrcp2', '18394338-9140-4950-8963-d1f4e543e1f8',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 22-year-old university student presents with a six-week history of auditory hallucinations, persecutory delusions about being monitored by classmates, social withdrawal and functional decline, with no prior psychiatric history and no substance misuse on urine toxicology. He is physically well.',
  'What is the most appropriate initial management approach?',
  array['first-episode psychosis','early intervention service','antipsychotic side-effect considerations']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000036', 'mrcp2', '18394338-9140-4950-8963-d1f4e543e1f8',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 38-year-old woman with schizophrenia has had an inadequate response, with persistent prominent auditory hallucinations and functional impairment, despite two separate adequate trials (correct dose, at least 6 weeks each, confirmed adherence) of different antipsychotics, most recently risperidone.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['treatment-resistant schizophrenia','clozapine initiation criteria','antipsychotic trial adequacy']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000037', 'mrcp2', '18394338-9140-4950-8963-d1f4e543e1f8',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 46-year-old woman with schizophrenia on long-term risperidone reports six months of secondary amenorrhoea, galactorrhoea and new-onset low libido. Serum prolactin is markedly elevated at 3200 mU/L (normal range up to around 500 mU/L in women), and pregnancy test is negative. Pituitary MRI, arranged given the degree of elevation, is normal.',
  'What is the most appropriate management?',
  array['antipsychotic-induced hyperprolactinaemia','aripiprazole switch','prolactin monitoring']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000038', 'mrcp2', '18394338-9140-4950-8963-d1f4e543e1f8',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 24-year-old man is started on IM haloperidol for acute agitation associated with a first episode of psychosis. Four hours later he develops sudden onset of neck twisting to one side with the head held in a fixed, painful position, and difficulty speaking. He is distressed but alert and has no fever.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate treatment?',
  array['acute dystonia','procyclidine','antipsychotic extrapyramidal side effect']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000039', 'mrcp2', '18394338-9140-4950-8963-d1f4e543e1f8',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'An 82-year-old man with probable dementia with Lewy bodies (parkinsonism, fluctuating cognition, visual hallucinations) is admitted with agitation and visual hallucinations of intruders in his room, distressing him significantly. A junior doctor considers starting haloperidol for symptom control.',
  'What is the most appropriate approach to this presentation?',
  array['Lewy body dementia','antipsychotic sensitivity','avoiding neuroleptics']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000040', 'mrcp2', '18394338-9140-4950-8963-d1f4e543e1f8',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 79-year-old woman is admitted with a fractured neck of femur and develops new visual hallucinations of small animals on the ward two days post-operatively, with fluctuating attention and disorientation to time and place, worse at night. She has no prior psychiatric history. Bloods show a raised white cell count and CRP, and urinalysis suggests a urinary tract infection; she is also mildly hypoxic on air.',
  'What is the most appropriate initial approach to this presentation?',
  array['delirium versus primary psychosis','reversible precipitants','avoiding unnecessary antipsychotic']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000041', 'mrcp2', '8ac8801d-f8de-400e-bee0-6d444b785d5d',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 49-year-old man with a 20-year history of heavy daily alcohol use is admitted with a two-day history of tremor, sweating, tachycardia and, overnight, visual hallucinations of insects crawling on the walls and marked agitation and disorientation. His last drink was 60 hours ago. Heart rate is 128/min, blood pressure 168/98 mmHg, and he is diaphoretic.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['delirium tremens','chlordiazepoxide','Pabrinex before glucose']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000042', 'mrcp2', '8ac8801d-f8de-400e-bee0-6d444b785d5d',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 33-year-old man is found unresponsive by paramedics with pinpoint pupils, a respiratory rate of 4 breaths/min and oxygen saturations of 82% on air. Track marks are noted on both antecubital fossae. He is known to use long-acting methadone in addition to injecting heroin intermittently.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management, including consideration of ongoing observation?',
  array['opioid overdose','naloxone','re-sedation risk with long-acting opioids']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000043', 'mrcp2', '8ac8801d-f8de-400e-bee0-6d444b785d5d',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 27-year-old man with opioid dependence, injecting heroin daily for three years, is referred for opioid substitution therapy. He has no significant cardiac history but a baseline ECG shows a QTc of 445 ms. He has stable housing and wants to reduce his risk of overdose from illicit heroin, though he is anxious about attending daily supervised consumption initially.',
  'What is the most appropriate initial choice of opioid substitution therapy?',
  array['opioid substitution therapy','buprenorphine versus methadone','QTc considerations']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000044', 'mrcp2', '8ac8801d-f8de-400e-bee0-6d444b785d5d',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 21-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department unresponsive after a mixed overdose of diazepam and an unknown quantity of alcohol, taken with a note expressing intent to die. Glasgow Coma Scale is 8/15 with a patent airway, adequate oxygen saturations, and no signs of opioid co-ingestion. She has a known history of epilepsy on regular carbamazepine.',
  'What is the most appropriate approach to reversal agents in this situation?',
  array['benzodiazepine overdose','flumazenil caution','seizure risk in mixed overdose']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000045', 'mrcp2', '8ac8801d-f8de-400e-bee0-6d444b785d5d',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 30-year-old man presents to the emergency department with severe central chest pain, sweating and palpitations two hours after using cocaine at a party. ECG shows ST elevation in the anterior leads, and troponin is subsequently elevated. Heart rate is 118/min and blood pressure 172/104 mmHg.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management, considering drug choice?',
  array['cocaine-induced ACS','avoiding beta-blockers','benzodiazepines and GTN']
),
('dc000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000046', 'mrcp2', '8ac8801d-f8de-400e-bee0-6d444b785d5d',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 58-year-old man with a long history of heavy alcohol use is admitted with confusion, ataxia and diplopia due to a bilateral sixth nerve palsy. He has not eaten properly for several days. Blood glucose is low-normal, and he appears malnourished.',
